<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <!--
      WARNING: do NOT re-add SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW here.

      It was added in v1.0.5 for the sync-progress overlay and is the prime
      suspect for the regression that build caused: the on-page SYNC button
      stopped receiving taps entirely (and so the post-sync dialog never
      appeared), while the in-plugin Settings sync kept working. That points at
      the GLOBAL hook (registerMotionListener) being withheld, which a host
      that sandboxes plugins could plausibly do to a plugin asking for a
      sensitive permission the host itself may not hold.

      It also buys us nothing. This APK is never installed as an app; the code
      runs inside com.ratta.supernote.pluginhost's process, so
      Settings.canDrawOverlays() reads the HOST's permission and our manifest
      governs nothing. If the overlay is ever built, the permission has to come
      from the host, not from here.

      (Keep prose here free of a doubled hyphen: XML forbids it inside a
      comment, and the manifest merger fails with a bare "Error parsing
      AndroidManifest.xml" that names no line.)
    -->

    <!--
      android:usesCleartextTraffic: every LAN backend (mac-server,
      google-tasks-server, ticktick-server) speaks plain http://, never
      https://, and targetSdkVersion 35 blocks cleartext traffic by default
      (Android 9+/API 28+) unless an app opts in. Device-reported 2026-08-16:
      TWO users, two different Supernote models (Nomad, Manta), two different
      backends (Google Tasks, Apple Reminders), reboot and network changes
      ruled out, and debug logs showed NO request ever reaching the server
      on ANY port when Sync/Find Server ran from the plugin, which only
      happens when a request is blocked before it leaves the device. This is
      the best candidate found so far.

      UNPROVEN, same caveat as the SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW note above: this
      manifest may govern nothing, since the plugin's code runs inside
      com.ratta.supernote.pluginhost's process rather than as its own
      installed app. Cheap and harmless to try either way, needs a real
      device test to confirm.
    -->
    <application
      android:name=".MainApplication"
      android:label="@string/app_name"
      android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
      android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
      android:allowBackup="false"
      android:theme="@style/AppTheme"
      android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"
      android:supportsRtl="true">
      <activity
        android:name=".MainActivity"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:configChanges="keyboard|keyboardHidden|orientation|screenLayout|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|uiMode"
        android:launchMode="singleTask"
        android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
        android:exported="true">
        <intent-filter>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent-filter>
      </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
